# Repo hygiene — retired proper nouns, as SHA-256 digests. Never plain text.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE "FIXING" THIS FILE.
#
# These are hashes and not names on purpose, and converting them back to plain
# text would undo the change that created this file.
#
# #211 removed a real host name from eight files in this public repo. A
# deny-list that spells the string it forbids puts it straight back: after such
# a change `git grep -i <name>` on the public tree still returns a hit, and
# "it is only in the linter" is a distinction that reads fine to us and not at
# all to someone scraping public repos for infrastructure names. The guard has
# to recognise the name without disclosing it, and a digest does exactly that.
#
# So: scripts/hygiene.denylist holds SHAPES in plain text, because a shape
# discloses nothing. This file holds NAMES as digests, because a name does.
# The two lists differ for that reason and should not be merged.
#
# HOW IT MATCHES
#   scripts/hygiene.py splits every tracked file into candidate word tokens
#   (also splitting camelCase and letter/digit boundaries, so a name embedded
#   in an identifier is still seen), lowercases each one, and compares its
#   SHA-256 against this list. Matching is therefore on whole tokens: a name is
#   caught inside a path, a URL, a hostname or an identifier, but an unrelated
#   word that merely CONTAINS one is not. That is what lets a premises name
#   scrubbed by #147 live here even though `covered` and `Covenant` appear in
#   LICENSE and CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md — #147 had to exclude those two files by
#   name, and this no longer does.
#
# HOW TO ADD ONE
#   printf '%s' "$(printf '%s' 'TheName' | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z')" | shasum -a 256
#   Add the digest and a label that describes the CLASS of the name, never the
#   name. Do not commit the plaintext anywhere — not in the label, not in the
#   commit message, not in the PR.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
#   Tokens too generic to hash safely: a name whose parts are ordinary words
#   ("nas", "seed", "mac", "mini", "ridge") would match legitimate prose on
#   every run, and a guard that cries wolf gets disabled. Those are covered by
#   the path and address shapes in scripts/hygiene.denylist instead, which is
#   where such a name actually shows up — inside a path, a URL or an ssh
#   command.
#
# FORMAT
#   <sha256 of the lowercased token>  <label describing the class, not the name>

d6f12e4e2cb4b9fb9d3c4a498a2ea0f5456e4a187a15632b2c368dc40c86de96  retired reference host name (#211)
5463f6e63b69781c30c7ea693229e4446e1f5623bbe4e66fd7ac64d9e0e3f0cd  premises name, scrubbed by #147
